    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Although not all studies of the mechanical behavior of faults include non-planar geometries, all natural faults have geometric irregularities. We add field observations and numerical modeling results to the mounting evidence suggesting that fault surface irregularities cause local variations in the slip and opening distributions along the fault, and perturbs local stress fields. We use a two-dimensional displacement discontinuity boundary element model in conjunction with a complementarity algorithm to predict the style and spatial distribution of off-fault damage from resultant local stress perturbations. We compare the numerical results to field observations of exhumed small faults in granodiorite from the central Sierra Nevada, California. This study focuses on fault opening, since evidence from the Sierra Nevada shows that these areas become conduits for hydrothermal fluid flow and are often surrounded by highly damaged wall rock due to chemical alteration and secondary fracturing. Numerical experiments along simplified curved model faults demonstrate that fault opening can occur along lee sides of the curves. Less compressive stress concentrates along lee sides and may be tensile enough to initiate open-mode fracturing, while more compressive stress concentrates adjacent to stoss sides of curves. Including a compressive internal normal stress along the model faults in order to mimic fluid pressure will increase the aperture and occurrence of opening and decrease the effective compressive stress along lee sides. While including variable frictional strength or internal normal stress along a planar fault may produce opening or perturb the local stresses, these devices do not accurately mimic the mechanical behavior we predict for faults with curved geometries.",
